WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.195
Authentication answers the question, who is making this request.

2
00:00:04.195 --> 00:00:05.506
In ASP.

3
00:00:05.506 --> 00:00:09.963
NET Core, authentication usually happens through configured schemes.

4
00:00:09.963 --> 00:00:12.454
A browser application may use cookies.

5
00:00:12.454 --> 00:00:14.355
An API may use bearer tokens.

6
00:00:14.355 --> 00:00:18.222
An enterprise application may rely on an external provider.

7
00:00:18.222 --> 00:00:26.546
A single application can support more than one scheme, but the team should understand which endpoints use which scheme and why.

8
00:00:26.546 --> 00:00:38.017
Cookie authentication is common for server-rendered browser applications because the browser can send the authentication cookie automatically with requests to the application.

9
00:00:38.017 --> 00:00:43.785
Bearer tokens are common for APIs because clients can present a token with each request.

10
00:00:43.785 --> 00:00:52.044
External providers can centralize identity, federation, multifactor enrollment, account lifecycle, or organizational policies.

11
00:00:52.044 --> 00:00:55.321
Each model has different operational expectations.

12
00:00:55.321 --> 00:00:56.632
ASP.

13
00:00:56.632 --> 00:01:00.761
NET Core Identity is a framework option for account management.

14
00:01:00.761 --> 00:01:08.889
It can support users, password workflows, claims, roles, sign-in, token generation, account confirmation, and related flows.

15
00:01:08.889 --> 00:01:21.474
It is useful when the application owns accounts directly, but teams still need to configure it carefully, review lifecycle paths, and decide how it fits with organizational identity practices.

16
00:01:21.474 --> 00:01:23.834
Authentication is not authorization.

17
00:01:23.834 --> 00:01:29.930
A signed-in user is identified, but identification does not answer what that user may access.

18
00:01:29.930 --> 00:01:39.303
The application still needs access rules for endpoints, records, tenant data, administrative workflows, exports, and state-changing operations.

19
00:01:39.303 --> 00:01:44.481
Treating login as blanket permission is one of the most common design mistakes.

20
00:01:44.481 --> 00:01:48.545
Engineering mistakes often come from trusting state too early.

21
00:01:48.545 --> 00:01:52.674
A page that shows a login form does not protect every endpoint.

22
00:01:52.674 --> 00:01:57.131
A local development identity does not represent production identity.

23
00:01:57.131 --> 00:02:00.736
A username in a request value is not proof of identity.

24
00:02:00.736 --> 00:02:04.735
A remembered session may not match current risk expectations.

25
00:02:04.735 --> 00:02:11.748
Authentication state should come from the configured authentication system, not from informal request data.

26
00:02:11.748 --> 00:02:15.419
Logout and session behavior also need production review.

27
00:02:15.419 --> 00:02:29.577
Teams should understand cookie lifetime, sliding expiration, revocation expectations, sign-out behavior, external provider logout where relevant, and what happens when roles or claims change during an active session.

28
00:02:29.577 --> 00:02:34.493
The user's identity lifecycle continues after the first successful sign-in.
